    "collected_at": "2026-10-14T18:16:35.931913+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://electrek.co/2026/10/14/volvo-delays-ex60-ev-deliveries-us/",
    "canonical_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/10/14/volvo-delays-ex60-ev-deliveries-us/",
    "origin_publisher": "Electrek",
    "published_at": "2026-10-14T16:19:17+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-14T16:19:17+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-14T18:17:38.940046+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-14T18:17:38.940046+00:00",
    "body": "Volvo is already calling the EX60 a “game changer” as its highest-range, fastest-charging EV to date. After delaying US deliveries, Volvo’s new midsize electric SUV will remain limited, at least for now.\nVolvo delays EX60 deliveries in the US\nVolvo’s new midsize electric SUV is impressive. As the first model built on its new SPA3 platform, the EX60 can drive further and charge faster than any all-electric Volvo so far.\nAfter delivering the first models in Europe this summer, Volvo said it planned to ramp up production as it rolled out to new markets, including the US.\nVolvo expected to sell roughly 7,000 EX60s in the US by the end of 2026, a dealer told Automotive News , but the company is on pace to deliver just a fraction of that.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThe Swedish luxury brand told dealers in a memo sent in late September that it had delayed customer deliveries, which Automotive News reviewed.\n“Introducing a brand-new platform and product into a running factory is always a challenge,” Volvo’s memo said, adding that “The delays are not related to vehicle quality, safety, or product performance.”\nVolvo EX60 start of production event in April (Source: Volvo)\nCiting a person with knowledge of the situation, the report claims Volvo is working through a “production crisis” that will leave the US with limited inventory until early 2027.\nVolvo spokesperson Jacquelyn Johnson confirmed that EX60 availability will “remain limited” in the short term. However, Johnson added that Volvo expects to distribute “a significant number” of EX60s to dealers in early 2027.\nThe source said Volvo will focus on deliveries in Europe, where EV sales are stronger, leaving the US on the back burner.\nVolvo EX60 reveal event (Source: Volvo)\nSince Volvo is using new manufacturing methods, such as megacasting, cell-to-body, and more, “the ramp-up phase takes a little longer than initially planned,” Johnson said.\nThe issue reportedly stems from integrating the EX60’s battery pack into the body structure and manufacturing the electric motors.\nVolvo began EX60 production in April at its upgraded Torslanda plant in Gothenburg, Sweden. Initial plans called for building around 2,000 EX60s a week, but the source said output will be about 10% of that.\nVolvo EX60 interior (Source: Volvo)\nThe 2027 Volvo EX60 starts at $58,400 and offers an EPA-estimated range of up to 307 miles. With up to 320 kW, it can add up to 155 miles in just 10 minutes of charging.\nStarting at $60,750, the EX60 P10 Plus offers up to 330 miles of range. It can add up to 165 miles in 10 minutes with up to 370 kW.\n2027 Volvo EX60 model | Starting Price * | Driving Range | Charging Time (10% to 80%) | 10 minutes of Charging | Max Charge Rate\nEX60 P6 Plus | $58,400 | 307 miles | 16 minutes | up to 155 miles | 320 kW\nEX60 P6 Ultra | $65,000 | 307 miles | 16 minutes | up to 155 miles | 320 kW\nEX60 P10 AWD Plus | $60,750 | 330 miles | 16 minutes | up to 165 miles | 370 kW\nEX60 P10 AWD Ultra | $67,350 | 330 miles | 16 minutes | up to 165 miles | 370 kW\nEX60 P12 AWD | N/A | 400 miles | 19 minutes | up to 173 miles | 370 kW\n2027 Volvo EX60 prices, driving range, and charging by variant (*Excluding $1,395 destination fee)\nVolvo previously said a range-topping EX60 P12 AWD will go on sale later this year, offering an estimated range of up to 400 miles.\nMeasuring 189.1″ long, 81.4 wide, and 64.4 tall, with a wheelbase of 116.9″, the EX60 is about the size of a BMW’s new iX3 and the Tesla Model Y.\nElectrek’s Take\nVolvo’s production crisis will open the door for luxury rivals like BMW and Mercedes-Benz to gain a first-mover advantage with their next-generation electric vehicles already rolling out.\nThe Swedish automaker has a pattern of production and software snags that has hampered other EV launches, including its flagship EX90 SUV.\nAs likely its most important electric vehicle to date, Volvo aims to get it right the first time with the EX60.\nMeanwhile, Volvo’s global sales dropped 10% to 141,609 units in the third quarter. In the US, its sales fell 8.7% to 23,766 units.\nInterested in seeing what’s available near you? You can click here to find available 2027 Volvo EX60 models in your area (trusted partner link).\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on Twitter , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
